## U.S. Census Bureau

The geography builder uses:

- Census Bureau [national state-code
  reference](https://www2.census.gov/geo/docs/reference/state.txt);
- American Community Survey 2019–2023 5-Year Table-Based Summary File, table
  B01003, plus the 2018–2022 edition for three omitted places;
- ACS 2023 geography lookup (`Geos20235YR`);
- 2020 Census ZCTA-to-County, ZCTA-to-Place, and
  ZCTA-to-County-Subdivision relationship files;
- 2023 Gazetteer county and county-subdivision files.

These are works of the U.S. Census Bureau, a U.S. federal agency. The public-domain
status asserted here rests on 17 U.S.C. §§ 101 and 105. This repository is not
endorsed or certified by the U.S. Census Bureau.

## GeoNames — CC BY 4.0

Source: [GeoNames US postal-code export](https://download.geonames.org/export/zip/US.zip)

This product contains information from [GeoNames](https://www.geonames.org/).
Its [export terms](https://www.geonames.org/export/) make the data available
under the [Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International
license](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).

Modifications: county FIPS codes are re-derived from state and `admin2` fields;
records are combined with U.S. Census Bureau sources; Census-derived values take
precedence where sources overlap. GeoNames is used as a fallback for postal codes,
including some PO-box-only and unique ZIP codes, that Census ZCTAs do not model.
Redistributors of the derived geography files must preserve the applicable
GeoNames attribution and license notice. The builder pins the SHA-256 digest of
the audited download and fails closed on drift. Because the GeoNames URL is
mutable and the source archive is not bundled, that check detects a changed
download but does not guarantee future retrieval of the audited bytes.

